        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Muscle motor control studies have investigated changes in the Achilles tendon reflex as affected by exposure to microgravity.  Scientists have noted that the length and tension properties of muscle, as well as its structure, seem to play important roles in the changes in motor control induced by microgravity.  Generalized hyperreflexia of the Achilles tendon was reported following the long-duration Skylab missions.  It was hypothesized that short-duration missions would produce same result.  The objective of this investigation was to test for changes in the neuromuscular function of the Achilles tendon reflex after the short-duration Apollo-Soyuz Test Project mission.  
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
Measurement of Achilles tendon reflex time was performed during the physical examination on all three prime astronauts at F-30 (30 days prior to launch), F-15, and on recovery day (R+0) using a Burdick FM-1 photomotograph.  A technique using a photoelectric cell was employed to time the Achilles tendon reflex by measuring the displacement of the foot.  The subject was instructed to kneel on a specially designed chair and the unit was positioned so that the light beam was partially intercepted by the metatarsal region of the foot.  The observer then tapped the Achilles tendon with a percussion hammer and thereby caused the foot to move in the light beam and generate a change in photocell voltage.  The change in voltage was recorded to give a time-position plot of the reflex action.  To determine the duration of the reflex response in milliseconds, measurements were made from the beginning of the hammer tap to the middle of the relaxation period.  
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
Four preflight baseline sets of data were obtained on the command module pilot (CMP) because he had previous tests conducted while serving as a backup crewmember for Skylab missions three and 4.  However, the commander (CDR) and the docking module pilot (DMP) only performed the test on F-30, F-15, and R+0.  The reflex was measured within 2 hours after recovery on R+0.  The CDR and the DMP exhibited a shorter reflex duration time, whereas the CMP showed a longer reflex time when the data was compared with the preflight results.  In addition to the noted changes in reflex time, all three crewmembers showed significant fine tremors, which could reflect the effects of the nitrogen tetroxide vapor, which was accidentally inhaled.  In general, the data showed the predicted postflight change in the Achilles tendon reflex time.
<END>
        </div>

